  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Gage Jump's 11 strikeouts bought them six innings to stay close and chase from behind.",
    "what_this_means": "Jump's dominance gave the lineup room to survive early pressure. Boston scored twice in the first inning, but the Athletics clawed back methodically. Two in the fifth to take the lead, two more in the sixth to build separation. This is the formula that works: the starter holds the door shut long enough for the offense to find its window, then the bullpen closes it. The lead changed hands once and never went back. Medina, Johnson, and Harris combined for 2.3 scoreless innings to lock it down. Three straight one-run wins is the trend now. Tight games where the margin never widens much but the team finds enough. That pattern holds if the pitching keeps doing this. If the starters falter, this team doesn't have room to breathe.",
